WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.715 --> 00:00:04.900
 If you type in relentless.com - it goes to the Amazon website. 

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:10.232
The online retailer’s growth has been relentless for 25 years, driven by Jeff Bezos.

00:00:11.638 --> 00:00:16.037
Yet his space company does not appear to be moving at the same pace. 

00:00:16.037 --> 00:00:19.730
Blue Origin sees itself as the tortoise in the race against the hare.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:22.863
The hare is so confident of winning it takes a nap. 

00:00:22.863 --> 00:00:26.029
The tortoise is slow and steady and ends up victorious. 

00:00:26.029 --> 00:00:29.261
But if SpaceX is the hare in this story, there's a twist. 

00:00:29.261 --> 00:00:32.227
Because it's definitely not napping.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:41.492
Bezos quietly founded Blue Origin in 2000. 

00:00:41.492 --> 00:00:44.325
Elon Musk started SpaceX two years later. 

00:00:44.325 --> 00:00:47.624
The entrepreneurs have been obsessed with space since they were young 

00:00:47.624 --> 00:00:50.257
but have radically different visions. 

00:00:50.257 --> 00:00:53.557
Bezos dreams of space colonies holding a million people, 

00:00:53.557 --> 00:00:56.656
sustained by the sun and the resources of the moon. 

00:00:56.656 --> 00:00:59.389
These are beautiful. People are gonna want to live here,

00:00:59.389 --> 00:01:02.421
 and they can be close to earth so that you can return.

00:01:02.421 --> 00:01:05.412
Before even trying to attempt floating colonies, 

00:01:05.412 --> 00:01:10.137
Blue Origin still needs to overcome the hurdle of actually getting into orbit first. 

00:01:12.353 --> 00:01:15.654
Different versions of the New Shepard have flown over a dozen times, 

00:01:15.654 --> 00:01:17.452
but it doesn’t enter orbit. 

00:01:17.452 --> 00:01:21.697
It flies past the Kármán line - the internationally recognized boundary of space,

00:01:21.697 --> 00:01:25.917
100 km or 62 miles out - and then it comes back down. 

00:01:25.917 --> 00:01:30.483
A short trip for future space tourists who have money to splurge on a joyride. 

00:01:30.483 --> 00:01:33.481
An orbital flight is much harder to achieve.

00:01:33.481 --> 00:01:36.548
Instead of going up and down, it goes around.

00:01:36.548 --> 00:01:38.777
This rocket is SpaceX’s Falcon 9. 

00:01:38.777 --> 00:01:41.847
It’s launched at a high enough speed to send it around the planet.

00:01:41.847 --> 00:01:44.846
SpaceX first went into orbit in 2008. 

00:01:44.846 --> 00:01:49.112
Blue Origin still hasn’t done the same 12 years later. 

00:01:49.112 --> 00:01:53.345
Musk once said this about his rival in an interview with the BBC. 

00:01:53.345 --> 00:01:59.310
You're obviously a very competitive person.
You're competing with the likes of Jeff Bezos, uh, in uh...

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:01.110
Jeff who?

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:04.576
Joking aside, he told the New York Times that Blue Origin’s 

00:02:04.576 --> 00:02:09.441
rate of progress is too slow and the amount of years Bezos has left is not enough.  

00:02:09.441 --> 00:02:14.859
The hope is for Bezos’ company to get into orbit in 2021 using New Glenn,

00:02:14.859 --> 00:02:16.407
a powerful rocket. 

00:02:16.407 --> 00:02:20.972
Just like SpaceX's suite of rockets, the New Glenn's booster lands back on Earth.

00:02:20.972 --> 00:02:24.172
It’s considerably bigger than SpaceX's Falcon Heavy

00:02:24.172 --> 00:02:28.571
but SpaceX is already developing a rocket that towers over them all.

00:02:28.571 --> 00:02:33.670
According to the company, Starship will be the world’s most powerful launch vehicle. 

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:39.569
It’s also fully reusable and will help fulfill Musk’s ambition of building a city on Mars. 

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:42.015
We need things that make you want to get out of bed in the morning

00:02:42.015 --> 00:02:43.368
 and be excited about the future. 

00:02:43.368 --> 00:02:46.543
And I think being a space-faring civilization is one of those things that

00:02:46.543 --> 00:02:48.467
 everyone can get excited about. 

00:02:49.367 --> 00:02:53.066
At least one person isn’t too excited about this particular plan.

00:02:53.066 --> 00:02:57.165
The Amazon boss calls the idea of colonizing mars “un-motivating”  

00:02:57.165 --> 00:03:00.486
and points out living on top of Mount Everest would be more comfortable

00:03:00.486 --> 00:03:01.798
 than a freezing Mars.

00:03:01.798 --> 00:03:03.798
Not that it deters Musk.

00:03:03.798 --> 00:03:08.696
He says there’s a fighting chance of an uncrewed mission to the red planet by 2024, 

00:03:08.696 --> 00:03:12.162
later than previously hoped, with a crewed mission to follow. 

00:03:12.162 --> 00:03:15.408
SpaceX made significant progress toward that goal 

00:03:15.408 --> 00:03:19.328
by sending astronauts to the International Space Station in May 2020, 

00:03:19.328 --> 00:03:22.360
the first private company to take people into orbit. 

00:03:22.794 --> 00:03:25.460
Working rapidly is engrained in the culture. 

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:29.825
Musk had millions when he started his rocket business but not billions. 

00:03:29.825 --> 00:03:33.335
Most of the parts are built in-house to cut down on costs. 

00:03:33.335 --> 00:03:36.985
Still, SpaceX nearly went bankrupt in 2008

00:03:36.985 --> 00:03:40.123
before a fourth attempt into orbit was finally successful 

00:03:40.123 --> 00:03:44.227
and helped land a $1.6 billion dollar contract with NASA.

00:03:44.227 --> 00:03:47.856
Pumping out products is necessary in order to secure partnerships

00:03:47.856 --> 00:03:50.358
 that will fund its biggest dreams.

00:03:50.358 --> 00:03:53.487
Blue Origin isn’t hurting for money right now.  

00:03:53.487 --> 00:03:56.417
Bezos is the richest person on the planet. 

00:03:56.417 --> 00:04:00.059
He sells more than a billion dollars worth of Amazon stock every year

00:04:00.059 --> 00:04:02.073
 and puts it toward his space venture. 

00:04:02.073 --> 00:04:05.358
An ex-girlfriend once said the reason he’s making so much money

00:04:05.358 --> 00:04:07.310
is to get to outer space.

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:10.196
Nowhere is the difference in their progress more stark 

00:04:10.196 --> 00:04:12.001
than when they go head to head.

00:04:12.001 --> 00:04:15.499
SpaceX beat out Blue Origin to win a contract worth billions 

00:04:15.499 --> 00:04:19.888
to launch classified missions for the U.S. Air Force using its Falcon rockets.

00:04:19.888 --> 00:04:22.896
The five-year deal begins in 2022.  

00:04:22.896 --> 00:04:26.140
Blue Origin said it was disappointed in the decision. 

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:30.322
The Pentagon also chose United Launch Alliance but not Northrop Grumman. 

00:04:30.322 --> 00:04:32.951
There are more races to come.

00:04:32.951 --> 00:04:36.354
Blue Origin, SpaceX, and Dynetics are designing landers

00:04:36.354 --> 00:04:38.264
for NASA’s next mission to the moon. 

00:04:38.264 --> 00:04:42.744
Bezos’ company has been replicating moon landings on Earth with New Shepard.  

00:04:42.744 --> 00:04:45.349
October’s flight carried sensors for the agency

00:04:45.349 --> 00:04:49.735
that will have the ability to look out for hazards like craters and large rocks.

00:04:49.735 --> 00:04:51.873
SpaceX has a very different approach.

00:04:51.873 --> 00:04:56.423
It wants to modify Starship instead of building a new spacecraft from scratch. 

00:04:56.423 --> 00:04:58.609
NASA says this could be game-changing 

00:04:58.609 --> 00:05:01.598
but questions whether the company can complete it in time 

00:05:01.598 --> 00:05:05.892
to carry the first woman and the next man to the moon by 2024.

00:05:05.892 --> 00:05:08.786
There is another arena in space where the two are competing.

00:05:08.786 --> 00:05:10.428
The internet space race. 

00:05:11.239 --> 00:05:12.798
Liftoff

00:05:14.005 --> 00:05:18.071
In my previous video, I talked about how SpaceX has been launching Starlink satellites 

00:05:18.071 --> 00:05:22.632
to connect places where internet is unreliable, unaffordable, or unavailable.

00:05:22.632 --> 00:05:27.207
Musk called Bezos a copycat for also creating a satellite internet service. 

00:05:27.207 --> 00:05:32.504
Amazon’s Project Kuiper, as it’s dubbed, could rely on Blue Origin for launches.  

00:05:32.504 --> 00:05:38.187
New Glenn is already lined up to deploy satellites for UK’s OneWeb and Canada's Telesat.  

00:05:38.187 --> 00:05:43.249
The US Federal Communications Commission approved Amazon’s 3,200 satellites

00:05:43.249 --> 00:05:47.940
so long as it doesn’t interfere with the 12,000-strong Starlink constellation

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:51.092
that could one day go up to 42,000.

00:05:51.092 --> 00:05:57.175
A  possible advantage Amazon has over SpaceX is that Kuiper can be integrated into the wider brand. 

00:05:57.175 --> 00:06:01.098
Amazon Web Services offers products such as data storage in the cloud.  

00:06:01.098 --> 00:06:03.717
Companies like Netflix are already clients, 

00:06:03.717 --> 00:06:06.980
so the satellites service can be the middleman to move the data. 

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:11.144
Except, we don’t know when launches will start or when the service will begin.

00:06:11.144 --> 00:06:15.605
Meanwhile, SpaceX has already put hundreds of satellites into low Earth orbit 

00:06:15.605 --> 00:06:20.862
and wants to provide near-global internet coverage by 2021 or 2022. 

00:06:22.025 --> 00:06:24.598
It appears SpaceX is well ahead of its competitor

00:06:24.598 --> 00:06:27.358
though Bezos does have a record of farsightedness.

00:06:27.358 --> 00:06:30.148
He’s trying to capture the spirit of thinking long-term

00:06:30.148 --> 00:06:35.678
by constructing a gigantic $42 million dollar clock in a mountain he owns in Texas 

00:06:35.678 --> 00:06:38.706
that’s supposed to tick for 10,000 years.

00:06:38.706 --> 00:06:43.261
But if you ask SpaceX, time is running out for Blue Origin.

00:06:43.261 --> 00:06:45.672
What do you think: Do you think Blue Origin will be able to

00:06:45.672 --> 00:06:49.019
make it into orbit in 2021? Do you think it'll happen after?

00:06:49.019 --> 00:06:49.988
Let me know.  

00:06:49.988 --> 00:06:51.368
If you like what you saw, give it a like

00:06:51.368 --> 00:06:53.262
And don't forget to subscribe to my channel.

00:06:53.262 --> 00:06:56.413
And thank you, as always, to my Patrons for your continued support. 

00:06:56.413 --> 00:06:57.705
I'll see you next time.

